One common way to introduce vulnerabilities in Java web applications is through **Unsafe Deserialization**. When an application uses `ObjectInputStream` to deserialize data provided by a user (such as via a cookie or a POST parameter), an attacker can craft a malicious serialized object to execute arbitrary code on the server (Remote Code Execution).

```java
import java.io.*;
import javax.servlet.http.*;

// This servlet demonstrates a vulnerable approach to handling serialized data
public class VulnerableServlet extends HttpServlet {
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws IOException {
        try {
            // Extracting a byte array from a request parameter (e.g., a base64 encoded object)
            byte[] data = Base64.getDecoder().decode(request.getParameter("objectData"));

            ByteArrayInputStream bais = new ByteArrayInputStream(data);
            // This line is vulnerable because it allows an attacker to instantiate 
            // any class available on the classpath during the deserialization process.
            ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(bais);

            // Deserializing the untrusted data directly
            Object obj = ois.readObject();
            response.getWriter().println("Received: " + obj.toString());
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```
***
```java
import java.io.*;
import javax.servlet.http.*;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;

// This servlet demonstrates a secure approach using JSON instead of native Java serialization
public class SecureServlet extends HttpServlet {
    // Using a standard JSON library like Jackson, which is data-only and does not 
    // instantiate arbitrary logic classes by default.
    private final ObjectMapper objectMapper = new ObjectMapper();

    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws IOException {
        try {
            // Instead of raw bytes, we expect a JSON string.
            // JSON parsing is safer because it maps data to specific, predefined POJOs.
            String jsonData = request.getParameter("jsonData");

            // Mapping the JSON to a specific, safe Data Transfer Object (DTO)
            // prevents the execution of malicious code during the parsing phase.
            UserDTO user = objectMapper.readValue(jsonData, UserDTO.class);

            response.getWriter().println("Received User: " + user.getName());
        } catch (Exception e) {
            response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST);
            response.getWriter().println("Invalid input format.");
        }
    }
}

// A simple POJO representing expected data structure
class UserDTO {
    private String name;
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }
}
```

